          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does low iron make people tired?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched explain part of the answer but not all of it.\n\n**What the sources say:** Hemoglobin is the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. Iron is needed to make it. When the body's iron stores run out, red cells are made smaller and paler than normal, which is the pattern seen on a blood count (Iron-deficiency anemia, General clinical reference).\n\n**What they do not say:** The sources do not explain how this leads to tiredness. My search for iron's role in cellular energy production returned nothing relevant, so I cannot describe that part from this material. The usual explanation is that less hemoglobin means less oxygen reaches the body's tissues, but the sources do not confirm this, so please treat it as unverified.\n\n**A point the sources do make:** In adults, iron is rarely lost except through bleeding. Low iron is therefore a finding that prompts a search for the cause, not a diagnosis in itself. Some causes need checking, including slow bleeding from the digestive tract (Iron-deficiency anemia). Seek urgent care right away if you notice vomiting blood or coffee-ground vomit, black tarry stools, heavy rectal bleeding, or any bleeding with dizziness, fainting, chest pain, or shortness of breath (Gastrointestinal bleeding).\n\nIf you are tired and wondering whether iron could be the cause, a clinician can order a blood count and iron tests and work out why the iron is low. Bring a list of your symptoms and any changes in bleeding or bowel habits.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
